ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":544,"index":544,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(76), start: 2, end: 3 })","issues":"[]","pages":[23],"side":0,"text":"Figure 4: Digital Signature Verification and Validation "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":545,"index":545,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[23],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":546,"index":546,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(77), start: 0, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[23],"side":0,"text":"  "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":547,"index":547,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(77), start: 2, end: 3 })","issues":"[]","pages":[23],"side":0,"text":"14 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":548,"index":548,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(78), start: 0, end: 1 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":549,"index":549,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(78), start: 1, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":0,"text":"4 The Digital Signature Algorithm (DSA) "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":550,"index":550,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(78), start: 2, end: 3 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":0,"text":"4.1 DSA Parameters "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":551,"index":551,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(78), start: 3, end: 8 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":0,"text":"A DSA digital signature is computed using a set of domain parameters, a private key x, a permessage secret number k, data to be signed, and a hash function. A digital signature is verified using the same domain parameters, a public key y that is mathematically associated with the private key x used to generate the digital signature, data to be verified, and the same hash function that was used during signature generation. These parameters are defined as follows: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":552,"index":552,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(78), start: 8, end: 10 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":0,"text":"p a prime modulus, where 2L–1 < p < 2L, and L is the bit length of p. Values for L are provided in Section 4.2. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":553,"index":553,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(78), start: 10, end: 12 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":0,"text":"q a prime divisor of (p – 1), where 2N–1 < q < 2 N, and N is the bit length of q. Values for N are provided in Section 4.2. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":554,"index":554,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(78), start: 12, end: 14 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":0,"text":"g a generator of a subgroup of order q in the multiplicative group of GF(p), such that 1 < g < p. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":555,"index":555,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(78), start: 14, end: 15 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":0,"text":"x the private key that must remain secret; x is a randomly or pseudorandomly generated "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":556,"index":556,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(78), start: 15, end: 16 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":0,"text":"integer, such that 0 < x < q, i.e., x is in the range [1, q–1]. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":557,"index":557,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(78), start: 16, end: 18 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":0,"text":"y the public key, where y = gx mod p. k a secret number that is unique to each message; k is a randomly or pseudorandomly "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":558,"index":558,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(78), start: 18, end: 19 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":0,"text":"generated integer, such that 0 < k < q, i.e., k is in the range [1, q–1]. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":559,"index":559,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(78), start: 19, end: 20 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":0,"text":"4.2 Selection of Parameter Sizes and Hash Functions for DSA "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":560,"index":560,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(78), start: 20, end: 22 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":0,"text":"This Standard specifies the following choices for the pair L and N (the bit lengths of p and q, respectively): "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":561,"index":561,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(78), start: 22, end: 26 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":0,"text":"L = 1024, N = 160 L = 2048, N = 224 L = 2048, N = 256 L = 3072, N = 256 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":562,"index":562,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(78), start: 26, end: 33 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":0,"text":"Federal Government entities shall generate digital signatures using use one or more of these choices. An approved hash function, as specified in FIPS 180, shall be used during the generation of digital signatures. The security strength associated with the DSA digital signature process is no greater than the minimum of the security strength of the (L, N) pair and the security strength of the hash function that is employed. Both the security strength of the hash function used and the security strength of the (L, N) pair shall meet or exceed the security strength required for the "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":563,"index":563,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":564,"index":564,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":565,"index":565,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":0,"text":"15 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":566,"index":566,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(79), start: 0, end: 26 })","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":0,"text":" digital signature process. The security strength for each (L, N) pair and hash function is provided in SP 800-57. SP 800-57 provides information about the selection of the appropriate (L, N) pair in accordance with a desired security strength for a given time period for the generation of digital signatures. An (L, N) pair shall be chosen that protects the signed information during the entire expected lifetime of that information. For example, if a digital signature is generated in 2009 for information that needs to be protected for five years, and a particular (L, N) pair is invalid after 2010, then a larger (L, N) pair shall be used that remains valid for the entire period of time that the information needs to be protected. It is recommended that the security strength of the (L, N) pair and the security strength of the hash function used for the generation of digital signatures be the same unless an agreement has been made between participating entities to use a stronger hash function. When the length of the output of the hash function is greater than N (i.e., the bit length of q), then the leftmost N bits of the hash function output block shall be used in any calculation using the hash function output during the generation or verification of a digital signature. A hash function that provides a lower security strength than the (L, N) pair ordinarily should not be used, since this would reduce the security strength of the digital signature process to a level no greater than that provided by the hash function. A Federal Government entity other than a Certification Authority (CA) should use only the first three (L, N) pairs (i.e., the (1024, 160), (2048, 224) and (2048, 256) pairs). A CA shall use an (L, N) pair that is equal to or greater than the (L, N) pairs used by its subscribers. For example, if subscribers are using the (2048, 224) pair, then the CA shall use either the (2048, 224), (2048, 256) or (3072, 256) pair. Possible exceptions to this rule include cross certification between CAs, certifying keys for purposes other than digital signatures and transitioning from one key size or algorithm to another. See SP 800-57 for further guidance. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":567,"index":567,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(79), start: 26, end: 27 })","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":0,"text":"4.3 DSA Domain Parameters "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":568,"index":568,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(79), start: 27, end: 38 })","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":0,"text":"DSA requires that the private/public key pairs used for digital signature generation and verification be generated with respect to a particular set of domain parameters. These domain parameters may be common to a group of users and may be public. A user of a set of domain parameters (i.e., both the signatory and the verifier) shall have assurance of their validity prior to using them (see Section 3). Although domain parameters may be public information, they shall be managed so that the correct correspondence between a given key pair and its set of domain parameters is maintained for all parties that use the key pair. A set of domain parameters may remain fixed for an extended time period. The domain parameters for DSA are the integers p, q, and g, and optionally, the domain_parameter_seed and counter that were used to generate p and q (i.e., the full set of domain parameters is (p, q, g {, domain_parameter_seed, counter})). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":569,"index":569,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":570,"index":570,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":571,"index":571,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":0,"text":"16 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":572,"index":572,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(80), start: 0, end: 1 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":573,"index":573,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(80), start: 1, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":0,"text":"4.3.1 Domain Parameter Generation "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":574,"index":574,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(80), start: 2, end: 8 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":0,"text":"Domain parameters may be generated by a trusted third party (a TTP, such as a CA) or by an entity other than a TTP. Assurance of domain parameter validity shall be obtained prior to key pair generation, digital signature generation or digital signature verification (see Section 3). The integers p and q shall be generated as specified in Appendix A.1. The input to the generation process is the selected values of L and N (see Section 4.2); the output of the generation process is the values for p and q, and optionally, the values of the "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":575,"index":575,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(80), start: 8, end: 9 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":0,"text":"domain_parameter_seed and counter. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":576,"index":576,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(80), start: 9, end: 14 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":0,"text":"The generator g shall be generated as specified in Appendix A.2. The security strength of a hash function used during the generation of the domain parameters shall meet or exceed the security strength associated with the (L, N) pair. Note that this is more restrictive than the hash function that can be used for the digital signature process (see Section 4.2). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":577,"index":577,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(80), start: 14, end: 15 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":0,"text":"4.3.2 Domain Parameter Management "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":578,"index":578,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(80), start: 15, end: 23 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":0,"text":"Each digital signature key pair shall be correctly associated with one specific set of domain parameters (e.g., by a public key certificate that identifies the domain parameters associated with the public key). The domain parameters shall be protected from unauthorized modification until the set is deactivated (if and when the set is no longer needed). The same domain parameters may be used for more than one purpose (e.g., the same domain parameters may be used for both digital signatures and key establishment). However, using different values for the generator g reduces the risk that key pairs generated for one purpose could be accidentally used (successfully) for another purpose. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":579,"index":579,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(80), start: 23, end: 24 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":0,"text":"4.4 Key Pairs "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":580,"index":580,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(80), start: 24, end: 30 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":0,"text":"Each signatory has a key pair: a private key x and a public key y that are mathematically related to each other. The private key shall be used for only a fixed period of time (i.e., the private key cryptoperiod) in which digital signatures may be generated; the public key may continue to be used as long as digital signatures that were generated using the associated private key need to be verified (i.e., the public key may continue to be used beyond the cryptoperiod of the associated private key). See SP 800-57 for further guidance. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":581,"index":581,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(80), start: 30, end: 31 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":0,"text":"4.4.1 DSA Key Pair Generation "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":582,"index":582,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(80), start: 31, end: 34 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":0,"text":"A digital signature key pair x and y is generated for a set of domain parameters (p, q, g {, domain_parameter_seed, counter}). Methods for the generation of x and y are provided in Appendix B.1. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":583,"index":583,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":584,"index":584,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":585,"index":585,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":0,"text":"17 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":586,"index":586,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 0, end: 1 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":587,"index":587,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 1, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"4.4.2 Key Pair Management "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":588,"index":588,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 2, end: 4 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"Guidance on the protection of key pairs is provided in SP 800-57. The secure use of digital signatures depends on the management of an entity’s digital signature key pair as follows: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":589,"index":589,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 4, end: 5 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":" 1. The validity of the domain parameters shall be assured prior to the generation of the key "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":590,"index":590,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 5, end: 6 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"pair, or the verification and validation of a digital signature (see Section 3). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":591,"index":591,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 6, end: 8 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"2. Each key pair shall be associated with the domain parameters under which the key pair was generated. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":592,"index":592,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 8, end: 10 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"3. A key pair shall only be used to generate and verify signatures using the domain parameters associated with that key pair. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":593,"index":593,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 10, end: 11 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"4. The private key shall be used only for signature generation as specified in this Standard "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":594,"index":594,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 11, end: 13 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"and shall be kept secret; the public key shall be used only for signature verification and may be made public. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":595,"index":595,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 13, end: 14 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"5. An intended signatory shall have assurance of possession of the private key prior to or "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":596,"index":596,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 14, end: 15 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"concurrently with using it to generate a digital signature (see Section 3.1). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":597,"index":597,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 15, end: 17 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"6. A private key shall be protected from unauthorized access, disclosure and modification. 7. A public key shall be protected from unauthorized modification (including substitution). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":598,"index":598,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 17, end: 18 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"For example, public key certificates that are signed by a CA may provide such protection. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":599,"index":599,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 18, end: 19 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"8. A verifier shall be assured of a binding between the public key, its associated domain "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":600,"index":600,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 19, end: 20 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"parameters and the key pair owner (see Section 3). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":601,"index":601,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 20, end: 21 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"9. A verifier shall obtain public keys in a trusted manner (e.g., from a certificate signed by a "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":602,"index":602,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 21, end: 24 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"CA that the entity trusts, or directly from the intended or claimed signatory, provided that the entity is trusted by the verifier and can be authenticated as the source of the signed information that is to be verified). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":603,"index":603,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 24, end: 25 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"10. Verifiers shall be assured that the claimed signatory is the key pair owner, and that the "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":604,"index":604,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 25, end: 28 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"owner possessed the private key that was used to generate the digital signature at the time that the signature was generated (i.e., the private key that is associated with the public key that will be used to verify the digital signature) (see Section 3.3). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":605,"index":605,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 28, end: 30 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"11. A signatory and a verifier shall have assurance of the validity of the public key (see Sections 3.1 and 3.3). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":606,"index":606,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 30, end: 31 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"4.5 DSA Per-Message Secret Number "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":607,"index":607,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(81), start: 31, end: 35 })","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"A new secret random number k shall be generated prior to the generation of each digital signature for use during the signature generation process. This secret number shall be protected from unauthorized disclosure and modification. k−1 is the multiplicative inverse of k with respect to multiplication modulo q; i.e., 0 < k−1 < q "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":608,"index":608,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":609,"index":609,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":610,"index":610,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[27],"side":0,"text":"18 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":611,"index":611,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(82), start: 0, end: 7 })","issues":"[]","pages":[28],"side":0,"text":" and 1 = (k−1 k) mod q. This inverse is required for the signature generation process (see Section 4.6). A technique is provided in Appendix C.1 for deriving k−1 from k. k and k−1 may be pre-computed, since knowledge of the message to be signed is not required for the computations. When k and k−1 are pre-computed, their confidentiality and integrity shall be protected. Methods for the generation of the per-message secret number are provided in Appendix B.2. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":612,"index":612,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(82), start: 7, end: 8 })","issues":"[]","pages":[28],"side":0,"text":"4.6 DSA Signature Generation "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":613,"index":613,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(82), start: 8, end: 13 })","issues":"[]","pages":[28],"side":0,"text":"The intended signatory shall have assurances as specified in Section 3.1. Let N be the bit length of q. Let min(N, outlen) denote the minimum of the positive integers N and outlen, where outlen is the bit length of the hash function output block. The signature of a message M consists of the pair of numbers r and s that is computed according to the following equations: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":614,"index":614,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(82), start: 13, end: 16 })","issues":"[]","pages":[28],"side":0,"text":"r = (gk mod p) mod q. z = the leftmost min(N, outlen) bits of Hash(M). s = (k−1(z + xr)) mod q. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":615,"index":615,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(82), start: 16, end: 27 })","issues":"[]","pages":[28],"side":0,"text":"When computing s, the string z obtained from Hash(M) shall be converted to an integer. The conversion rule is provided in Appendix C.2. Note that r may be computed whenever k, p, q and g are available, e.g., whenever the domain parameters p, q and g are known, and k has been pre-computed (see Section 4.5), r may also be pre-computed, since knowledge of the message to be signed is not required for the computation of r. Pre-computed k, k-1 and r values shall be protected in the same manner as the the private key x until s has been computed (see SP 800-57). The values of r and s shall be checked to determine if r = 0 or s = 0. If either r = 0 or s = 0, a new value of k shall be generated, and the signature shall be recalculated. It is extremely unlikely that r = 0 or s = 0 if signatures are generated properly. The signature (r, s) may be transmitted along with the message to the verifier. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":616,"index":616,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(82), start: 27, end: 28 })","issues":"[]","pages":[28],"side":0,"text":"4.7 DSA Signature Verification and Validation "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":617,"index":617,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(82), start: 28, end: 31 })","issues":"[]","pages":[28],"side":0,"text":"Signature verification may be performed by any party (i.e., the signatory, the intended recipient or any other party) using the signatory’s public key. A signatory may wish to verify that the computed signature is correct, perhaps before sending the signed message to the intended "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":618,"index":618,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[28],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":619,"index":619,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[28],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":620,"index":620,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[28],"side":0,"text":"19 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":621,"index":621,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(83), start: 0, end: 12 })","issues":"[]","pages":[29],"side":0,"text":" recipient. The intended recipient (or any other party) verifies the signature to determine its authenticity. Prior to verifying the signature of a signed message, the domain parameters, and the claimed signatory’s public key and identity shall be made available to the verifier in an authenticated manner. The public key may, for example, be obtained in the form of a certificate signed by a trusted entity (e.g., a CA) or in a face-to-face meeting with the public key owner. Let M ′, r′, and s′ be the received versions of M, r, and s, respectively; let y be the public key of the claimed signatory; and let N be the bit length of q. Also, let min(N, outlen) denote the minimum of the positive integers N and outlen, where outlen is the bit length of the hash function output block. The signature verification process is as follows: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":622,"index":622,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(83), start: 12, end: 14 })","issues":"[]","pages":[29],"side":0,"text":"1. The verifier shall check that 0 < r′ < q and 0 < s′ < q; if either condition is violated, the signature shall be rejected as invalid. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":623,"index":623,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(83), start: 14, end: 24 })","issues":"[]","pages":[29],"side":0,"text":"2. If the two conditions in step 1 are satisfied, the verifier computes the following: w = (s′)–1 mod q. z = the leftmost min(N, outlen) bits of Hash(M′ ). u1 = (zw) mod q. u2 = ((r′)w) mod q. v = (((g)u1 (y)u2) mod p) mod q. A technique is provided in Appendix C.1 for deriving (s′ )–1 (i.e., the multiplicative inverse of s′ mod q). The string z obtained from Hash(M′) shall be converted to an integer. The conversion rule is provided in Appendix C.2. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":624,"index":624,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(83), start: 24, end: 26 })","issues":"[]","pages":[29],"side":0,"text":"3. If v = r′, then the signature is verified. For a proof that v = r′ when M′ = M, r′ = r, and s′ = s, see Appendix E. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":625,"index":625,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(83), start: 26, end: 27 })","issues":"[]","pages":[29],"side":0,"text":"4. If v does not equal r′, then the message or the signature may have been modified, there "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":626,"index":626,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(83), start: 27, end: 32 })","issues":"[]","pages":[29],"side":0,"text":"may have been an error in the signatory’s generation process, or an imposter (who did not know the private key associated with the public key of the claimed signatory) may have attempted to forge the signature. The signature shall be considered invalid. No inference can be made as to whether the data is valid, only that when using the public key to verify the signature, the signature is incorrect for that data. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":627,"index":627,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(83), start: 32, end: 34 })","issues":"[]","pages":[29],"side":0,"text":"5. Prior to accepting the signature as valid, the verifier shall have assurances as specified in Section 3.3. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":628,"index":628,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[29],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":629,"index":629,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[29],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":630,"index":630,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[29],"side":0,"text":"20 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":631,"index":631,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 0, end: 5 })","issues":"[]","pages":[30],"side":0,"text":" An organization’s policy may govern the action to be taken for invalid digital signatures. Such policy is outside the scope of this Standard. Guidance about determining the timeliness of digitally signed messages is addressed in SP 800-102, Recommendation for Digital Signature Timeliness. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":632,"index":632,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 0, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[30],"side":0,"text":"  "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":633,"index":633,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 2, end: 3 })","issues":"[]","pages":[30],"side":0,"text":"21 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":634,"index":634,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 0, end: 1 })","issues":"[]","pages":[31],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":635,"index":635,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 1, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[31],"side":0,"text":"5. The RSA Digital Signature Algorithm "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":636,"index":636,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 2, end: 7 })","issues":"[]","pages":[31],"side":0,"text":"The use of the RSA algorithm for digital signature generation and verification is specified in American National Standard (ANS) X9.31 and Public Key Cryptography Standard (PKCS) #1. While each of these standards uses the RSA algorithm, the format of the ANS X9.31 and PKCS #1 data on which the digital signature is generated differs in details that make the algorithms non-interchangeable. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":637,"index":637,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 7, end: 8 })","issues":"[]","pages":[31],"side":0,"text":"5.1 RSA Key Pair Generation "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":638,"index":638,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 8, end: 36 })","issues":"[]","pages":[31],"side":0,"text":"An RSA digital signature key pair consists of an RSA private key, which is used to compute a digital signature, and an RSA public key, which is used to verify a digital signature. An RSA key pair used for digital signatures shall only be used for one digital signature scheme (e.g., ANS X9.31, RSASSA-PKCS1 v1.5 or RSASSA-PSS; see Sections 5.4 and 5.5). In addition, an RSA digital signature key pair shall not be used for other purposes (e.g., key establishment). An RSA public key consists of a modulus n, which is the product of two positive prime integers p and q (i.e., n = pq), and a public key exponent e. Thus, the RSA public key is the pair of values (n, e) and is used to verify digital signatures. The size of an RSA key pair is commonly considered to be the length of the modulus n in bits (nlen). The corresponding RSA private key consists of the same modulus n and a private key exponent d that depends on n and the public key exponent e. Thus, the RSA private key is the pair of values (n, d) and is used to generate digital signatures. Note that an alternative method for representing (n, d) using the Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT) is allowed. In order to provide security for the digital signature process, the two integers p and q, and the private key exponent d shall be kept secret. The modulus n and the public key exponent e may be made known to anyone. Guidance on the protection of these values is provided in SP 800-57. This Standard specifies three choices for the length of the modulus (i.e., nlen): 1024, 2048 and 3072 bits. Federal Government entities shall generate digital signatures using one or more of these choices. An approved hash function, as specified in FIPS 180, shall be used during the generation of key pairs and digital signatures. When used during the generation of an RSA key pair (as specified in this Standard), the length in bits of the hash function output block shall meet or exceed the security strength associated with the bit length of the modulus n (see SP 800-57). The security strength associated with the RSA digital signature process is no greater than the minimum of the security strength associated with the bit length of the modulus and the security strength of the hash function that is employed. The security strength for each modulus length and hash function used during the digital signature process is provided in SP 800-57. Both the security strength of the hash function used and the security strength associated with the bit length "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":639,"index":639,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[31],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":640,"index":640,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[31],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":641,"index":641,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[31],"side":0,"text":"22 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":642,"index":642,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 0, end: 26 })","issues":"[]","pages":[32],"side":0,"text":" of the modulus n shall meet or exceed the security strength required for the digital signature process. It is recommended that the security strength of the modulus and the security strength of the hash function be the same unless an agreement has been made between participating entities to use a stronger hash function. A hash function that provides a lower security strength than the security strength associated with the bit length of the modulus ordinarily should not be used, since this would reduce the security strength of the digital signature process to a level no greater than that provided by the hash function. Federal Government entities other than CAs should use only the first two choices (i.e., nlen = 1024 or 2048) during the timeframes indicated in SP 800-57. A CA should use a modulus whose length nlen is equal to or greater than the moduli used by its subscribers. For example, if the subscribers are using nlen = 2048, then the CA should use nlen ≥ 2048. SP 800-57 provides further information about the selection of the bit length of n. Possible exceptions to this rule include cross certification between CAs, certifying keys for purposes other than digital signatures and transitioning from one key size or algorithm to another. Criteria for the generation of RSA key pairs are provided in Appendix B.3.1. When RSA parameters are randomly generated (i.e., the primes p and q, and optionally, the public key exponent e), they shall be generated using an approved random bit generator. The (pseudo) random bits produced by the random bit generator shall be used as seeds for generating RSA parameters (e.g., the (pseudo) random number is used as a prime number generation seed). Prime number generation seeds shall be kept secret or destroyed when the modulus n is computed. If any prime number generation seed is retained (e.g., to regenerate the RSA modulus n, or as evidence that the generated prime factors p and q were generated in compliance with this Standard, then the seed shall be kept secret and shall be protected. The strength of this protection shall be (at least) equivalent to the protection required for the associated private key. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":643,"index":643,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 26, end: 27 })","issues":"[]","pages":[32],"side":0,"text":"5.2 Key Pair Management "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":644,"index":644,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 27, end: 31 })","issues":"[]","pages":[32],"side":0,"text":"The secure use of digital signatures depends on the management of an entity’s digital signature key pair. Key pair management requirements for RSA are provided in Section 4.4.2, requirements 4 – 11. Note that the first three requirements in Section 4.4.2, which address the relationship between domain parameters and key pairs, are not applicable to RSA. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":645,"index":645,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 31, end: 32 })","issues":"[]","pages":[32],"side":0,"text":"5.3 Assurances "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":646,"index":646,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 32, end: 34 })","issues":"[]","pages":[32],"side":0,"text":"The intended signatory shall have assurances as specified in Section 3.1. Prior to accepting a digital signature as valid, the verifier shall have assurances as specified in Section 3.3. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":647,"index":647,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(88), start: 0, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[32],"side":0,"text":"  "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":648,"index":648,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(88), start: 2, end: 3 })","issues":"[]","pages":[32],"side":0,"text":"23 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":649,"index":649,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 0, end: 1 })","issues":"[]","pages":[33],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":650,"index":650,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 1, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[33],"side":0,"text":"5.4 ANS X9.31 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":651,"index":651,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 2, end: 3 })","issues":"[]","pages":[33],"side":0,"text":"ANS X9.31, Digital Signatures Using Reversible Public Key Cryptography for the Financial "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":652,"index":652,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 3, end: 18 })","issues":"[]","pages":[33],"side":0,"text":"Services Industry (rDSA), was developed for the American National Standards Institute by the Accredited Standards Committee on Financial Services, X9. See http://www.x9.org for information about obtaining copies of ANS X9.31 and any associated errata. The following discussions are based on the version of ANS X9.31 that was approved in 1998. Methods for the generation of the private prime factors p and q are provided in Appendix B.3. In ANS X9.31, the length of the modulus n is allowed in increments of 256 bits beyond a minimum of 1024 bits. Implementations claiming conformance to FIPS 186-4 shall include one or more of the modulus sizes specified in Section 5.1. Two methods for the generation of digital signatures are included in ANS X9.31. When the public signature verification exponent e is odd, the digital signature algorithm is commonly known as RSA; when the public signature verification exponent e is even, the digital signature algorithm is commonly known as Rabin-Williams. This Standard (i.e., FIPS 186-4) adopts the use of RSA, but does not adopt the use of Rabin-Williams. During signature verification, the extraction of the hash value H(M)′ from the data structure IR′ shall be accomplished by either: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":653,"index":653,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 18, end: 19 })","issues":"[]","pages":[33],"side":0,"text":"• Selecting the hashlen bytes of the data structure IR′ that immediately precedes the two "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":654,"index":654,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 19, end: 21 })","issues":"[]","pages":[33],"side":0,"text":"bytes of trailer information, where hashlen is the length in bytes of the hash function used, regardless of the length of the padding, or "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":655,"index":655,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 21, end: 22 })","issues":"[]","pages":[33],"side":0,"text":"• If the hash value H(M)′ is selected by its location with respect to the last byte of padding "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":656,"index":656,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 22, end: 24 })","issues":"[]","pages":[33],"side":0,"text":"(i.e., 0xBA), including a check that the hash value is followed by only two bytes containing the expected trailer value. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":657,"index":657,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 24, end: 28 })","issues":"[]","pages":[33],"side":0,"text":"ANS X9.31 contains an annex on random number generation. However, implementations of ANS X9.31 shall use approved random number generation methods. Annexes in ANS X9.31 provide informative discussions of security and implementation considerations. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":658,"index":658,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 28, end: 29 })","issues":"[]","pages":[33],"side":0,"text":"5.5 PKCS #1 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":659,"index":659,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 29, end: 34 })","issues":"[]","pages":[33],"side":0,"text":"Public-Key Cryptography Standard (PKCS) #1, RSA Cryptography Standard, defines mechanisms for encrypting and signing data using the RSA algorithm. PKCS #1 v2.1 specifies two digital signature processes and corresponding formats: RSASSA-PKCS1-v1.5 and RSASSA-PSS. Both signature schemes are approved for use, but additional constraints are imposed beyond those specified in PKCS #1 v2.1. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":660,"index":660,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 34, end: 36 })","issues":"[]","pages":[33],"side":0,"text":"(a) Implementations that generate RSA key pairs shall use the criteria and methods in Appendix B.3 to generate those key pairs, "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":661,"index":661,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[33],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":662,"index":662,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[33],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":663,"index":663,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[33],"side":0,"text":"24 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":664,"index":664,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(90), start: 0, end: 1 })","issues":"[]","pages":[34],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":665,"index":665,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(90), start: 1, end: 5 })","issues":"[]","pages":[34],"side":0,"text":"(b) Only approved hash functions shall be used. (c) Only two prime factors p and q shall be used to form the modulus n. (d) Random numbers shall be generated using an approved random bit generator. (e) For RSASSA-PSS: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":666,"index":666,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(90), start: 5, end: 6 })","issues":"[]","pages":[34],"side":0,"text":"• If nlen = 1024 bits (i.e., 128 bytes), and the output length of the approved hash "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":667,"index":667,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(90), start: 6, end: 8 })","issues":"[]","pages":[34],"side":0,"text":"function output block is 512 bits (i.e., 64 bytes), then the length (in bytes) of the salt (sLen) shall satisfy 0 ≤ sLen ≤ hLen – 2, "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":668,"index":668,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(90), start: 8, end: 9 })","issues":"[]","pages":[34],"side":0,"text":"• Otherwise, the length (in bytes) of the salt (sLen) shall satisfy 0 ≤ sLen ≤ hLen, "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":669,"index":669,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(90), start: 9, end: 10 })","issues":"[]","pages":[34],"side":0,"text":"where hLen is the length of the hash function output block (in bytes). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":670,"index":670,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(90), start: 10, end: 11 })","issues":"[]","pages":[34],"side":0,"text":"(f) For RSASSA-PKCS-v1.5, when the hash value is recovered from the encoded message "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":671,"index":671,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(90), start: 11, end: 14 })","issues":"[]","pages":[34],"side":0,"text":"EM during the verification of the digital signature1, the extraction of the hash value shall be accomplished by either: • Selecting the rightmost (least significant) bits of EM, based on the size of the hash "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":672,"index":672,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(90), start: 14, end: 15 })","issues":"[]","pages":[34],"side":0,"text":"function used, regardless of the length of the padding, or "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":673,"index":673,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(90), start: 15, end: 16 })","issues":"[]","pages":[34],"side":0,"text":"• If the hash value is selected by its location with respect to the last byte of padding, "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":674,"index":674,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(90), start: 16, end: 19 })","issues":"[]","pages":[34],"side":0,"text":"including a check that the hash value is located in the rightmost (least significant) bytes of EM (i.e., no other information follows the hash value in the encoded message). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":675,"index":675,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(90), start: 19, end: 22 })","issues":"[]","pages":[34],"side":0,"text":"Note: PKCS #1 was initially developed by RSA Laboratories in 1991 and has been revised as multiple versions. At the time of the approval of FIPS 186-4, three versions of PKSC #1 were available: version 1.5, version 2.0 and version 2.1. This Standard references only version 2.1. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":676,"index":676,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(91), start: 0, end: 4 })","issues":"[]","pages":[34],"side":0,"text":" 1 PKCS #1, v2.1 provides two methods for comparing the hash values: by comparing the encoded messages EM and EM′, and by extracting the hash value from the decoding of the encoded message (see the Note in PKCS #1, v2.1). Step (f) above applies to the latter case. "}
