ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":857,"index":857,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(107), start: 18, end: 19 })","issues":"[]","pages":[45],"side":0,"text":"A.1.2 Construction and Validation of the Provable Primes p and q "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":858,"index":858,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(107), start: 19, end: 28 })","issues":"[]","pages":[45],"side":0,"text":"Primes can be generated so that they are guaranteed to be prime. The following algorithm for generating p and q uses an approved hash function and an arbitrary seed (firstseed) to construct p and q in the required intervals. The security strength of the hash function shall be equal to or greater than the security strength associated with the (L, N) pair. Arbitrary seeds can be anything, e.g., a user’s favorite number or a random or pseudorandom number that is output from a random number generator. Note that the firstseed must be unique to produce a unique set of domain parameters. Candidate primes are tested for primality using a deterministic primality test that proves whether or not the candidate is prime. The resulting p and q are guaranteed to be primes. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":859,"index":859,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(107), start: 28, end: 29 })","issues":"[]","pages":[45],"side":0,"text":"A.1.2.1 Construction of the Primes p and q Using the Shawe-Taylor Algorithm "}
