ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1155,"index":1155,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(124), start: 27, end: 28 })","issues":"[]","pages":[59],"side":0,"text":"B.3 IFC Key Pair Generation "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1156,"index":1156,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(124), start: 28, end: 29 })","issues":"[]","pages":[59],"side":0,"text":"B.3.1 Criteria for IFC Key Pairs "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1157,"index":1157,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(124), start: 29, end: 32 })","issues":"[]","pages":[59],"side":0,"text":"Key pairs for IFC consist of a public key (n, e), and a private key (n, d), where n is the modulus and is the product of two prime numbers p and q. The security of IFC depends on the quality and secrecy of these primes and the private exponent d. The primes p and q shall be generated using "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1158,"index":1158,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[59],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1159,"index":1159,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[59],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1160,"index":1160,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[59],"side":0,"text":"50 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1161,"index":1161,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(125), start: 0, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[60],"side":0,"text":" one of the following methods: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1162,"index":1162,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(125), start: 2, end: 4 })","issues":"[]","pages":[60],"side":0,"text":"A. Both p and q are randomly generated prime numbers (Random Primes), where p and q shall both be either : "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1163,"index":1163,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(125), start: 4, end: 6 })","issues":"[]","pages":[60],"side":0,"text":"1. Provable primes (see Appendix B.3.2), or 2. Probable primes (see Appendix B.3.3). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1164,"index":1164,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(125), start: 6, end: 10 })","issues":"[]","pages":[60],"side":0,"text":"Using methods 1 and 2, p and q with lengths of 1024 or 1536 bits may be generated; p and q with lengths of 512 bits shall not be generated using these methods. Instead, p and q with lengths of 512 bits shall be generated using the conditions based on auxiliary primes (see Appendices B.3.4, B.3.5, or B.3.6). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1165,"index":1165,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(125), start: 10, end: 12 })","issues":"[]","pages":[60],"side":0,"text":"B. Both p and q are randomly generated prime numbers that satisfy the following additional conditions (Primes with Conditions): "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1166,"index":1166,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(125), start: 12, end: 16 })","issues":"[]","pages":[60],"side":0,"text":"• (p–1) has a prime factor p1 • (p+1) has a prime factor p2 • (q–1) has a prime factor q1 • (q+1) has a prime factor q2 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1167,"index":1167,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(125), start: 16, end: 18 })","issues":"[]","pages":[60],"side":0,"text":"where p1, p2, q1 and q2 are called auxiliary primes of p and q. Using this method, one of the following cases shall apply: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1168,"index":1168,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(125), start: 18, end: 20 })","issues":"[]","pages":[60],"side":0,"text":"1. The primes p1, p2, q1, q2, p and q shall all be provable primes (see Appendix B.3.4), "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1169,"index":1169,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(125), start: 20, end: 21 })","issues":"[]","pages":[60],"side":0,"text":"2. The primes p1, p2, q1 and q2 shall be provable primes, and the primes p and q "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1170,"index":1170,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(125), start: 21, end: 22 })","issues":"[]","pages":[60],"side":0,"text":"shall be probable primes (see Appendix B.3.5), or "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1171,"index":1171,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(125), start: 22, end: 24 })","issues":"[]","pages":[60],"side":0,"text":"3 The primes p1, p2, q1, q2, p and q shall all be probable primes (see Appendix B.3.6). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1172,"index":1172,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(125), start: 24, end: 32 })","issues":"[]","pages":[60],"side":0,"text":"The minimum lengths for each of the auxiliary primes p1, p2, q1 and q2 are dependent on nlen, where nlen is the length of the modulus n in bits. Note that nlen is also called the key size. The lengths of the auxiliary primes may be fixed or randomly chosen, subject to the restrictions in Table B.1. The maximum length is determined by nlen (the sum of the length of each auxiliary prime pair) and whether the primes p and q are probable primes or provable primes (e.g., for the auxiliary prime pair p1 and p2, len(p1) + len(p2) shall be less than a value determined by nlen, whether p1 and p2 are generated to be probable or provable primes)3. "}
