ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1752,"index":1752,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(177), start: 6, end: 10 })","issues":"[]","pages":[84],"side":0,"text":"Steps 6.2, 6.3.1 and 6.3.2 contain expressions of the form A/2 mod C, where A is an integer, and C is an odd integer. If A/2 is not an integer (i.e., A is odd), then A/2 mod C may be calculated as (A+C)/2 mod C. Alternatively, A/2 mod C = A·(C+1)/2 mod C, for any integer A, without regard to A being odd or even. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1753,"index":1753,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(177), start: 10, end: 11 })","issues":"[]","pages":[84],"side":0,"text":"C.4 Checking for a Perfect Square "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1754,"index":1754,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(177), start: 11, end: 13 })","issues":"[]","pages":[84],"side":0,"text":"The following algorithm may be used to determine whether an n-bit positive integer C is a perfect square: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1755,"index":1755,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(177), start: 13, end: 14 })","issues":"[]","pages":[84],"side":0,"text":"Input: "}
