ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1863,"index":1863,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(183), start: 0, end: 1 })","issues":"[]","pages":[89],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1864,"index":1864,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(183), start: 1, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[89],"side":0,"text":"28. prime_seed = prime_seed + iterations + 1. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1865,"index":1865,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(183), start: 2, end: 5 })","issues":"[]","pages":[89],"side":0,"text":"29. a = 2 + (a mod (c – 3)). 30. z = a2t mod c. 31. If ((1 = GCD(z – 1, c)) and (1 = zc0 mod c)), then "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1866,"index":1866,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(183), start: 5, end: 6 })","issues":"[]","pages":[89],"side":0,"text":"31.1 prime = c. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1867,"index":1867,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(183), start: 6, end: 7 })","issues":"[]","pages":[89],"side":0,"text":"31.2 Return (SUCCESS, prime, prime_seed {, prime_gen_counter}). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1868,"index":1868,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(183), start: 7, end: 9 })","issues":"[]","pages":[89],"side":0,"text":"32. If (prime_gen_counter ≥ ((4 ∗ length) + old_counter)), then return (FAILURE, 0, 0 {, 0}). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1869,"index":1869,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(183), start: 9, end: 11 })","issues":"[]","pages":[89],"side":0,"text":"33. t = t + 1. 34. Go to step 23. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1870,"index":1870,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(183), start: 11, end: 12 })","issues":"[]","pages":[89],"side":0,"text":"C.7 Trial Division "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1871,"index":1871,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(183), start: 12, end: 15 })","issues":"[]","pages":[89],"side":0,"text":"An integer is proven to be prime by showing that it has no prime factors less than or equal to its square root. This procedure is not recommended for testing any integers longer than 10 digits. To prove that c is prime: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1872,"index":1872,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(183), start: 15, end: 17 })","issues":"[]","pages":[89],"side":0,"text":"1. Prepare a table of primes less than c . This can be done by applying the sieve procedure in Appendix C.8. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1873,"index":1873,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(183), start: 17, end: 18 })","issues":"[]","pages":[89],"side":0,"text":"2. Divide c by every prime in the table. If c is divisible by one of the primes, then declare that c "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1874,"index":1874,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(183), start: 18, end: 21 })","issues":"[]","pages":[89],"side":0,"text":"is composite and exit. If convenient, c may be divided by composite numbers. For example, rather than preparing a table of primes, it might be more convenient to divide by all integers except those divisible by 3 or 5. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1875,"index":1875,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(183), start: 21, end: 22 })","issues":"[]","pages":[89],"side":0,"text":"3. Otherwise, declare that c is prime and exit. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1876,"index":1876,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(183), start: 22, end: 23 })","issues":"[]","pages":[89],"side":0,"text":"C.8 Sieve Procedure "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1877,"index":1877,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(183), start: 23, end: 32 })","issues":"[]","pages":[89],"side":0,"text":"A sieve procedure is described as follows: Given a sequence of integers Y0, Y0 + 1, … , Y0 + J, a sieve will identify the integers in the sequence that are divisible by primes up to some selected limit. Note that the definitions of the mathematical symbols in this process (e.g., h, L, M, p) are internal to this process only, and should not be confused with their use elsewhere in this Standard. Start by selecting a factor base of all the primes pj, from 2 up to some selected limit L. The value of L is arbitrary and may be determined by computer limitations. A good, typical value of L would be anywhere from 103 to 105. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1878,"index":1878,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[89],"side":0,"text":" "}
