ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":2011,"index":2011,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(197), start: 1, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[96],"side":0,"text":"Appendix D: Recommended Elliptic Curves for Federal "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":2012,"index":2012,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(197), start: 2, end: 3 })","issues":"[]","pages":[96],"side":0,"text":"Government Use "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":2013,"index":2013,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(197), start: 3, end: 4 })","issues":"[]","pages":[96],"side":0,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":2014,"index":2014,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(197), start: 4, end: 9 })","issues":"[]","pages":[96],"side":0,"text":"This collection of elliptic curves is recommended for Federal government use and contains choices for the private key length and underlying fields. These curves were generated using SHA-1 and the method given in the ANS X9.62 and IEEE Standard 1363-2000 standards. This appendix describes the process that was used. Note that these curves are the same as those included in the previous version of this Standard. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":2015,"index":2015,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(197), start: 9, end: 10 })","issues":"[]","pages":[96],"side":0,"text":"D.1 NIST Recommended Elliptic Curves "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":2016,"index":2016,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(197), start: 10, end: 11 })","issues":"[]","pages":[96],"side":0,"text":"D.1.1 Choices "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":2017,"index":2017,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(197), start: 11, end: 12 })","issues":"[]","pages":[96],"side":0,"text":"D.1.1.1 Choice of Key Lengths "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":2018,"index":2018,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(197), start: 12, end: 18 })","issues":"[]","pages":[96],"side":0,"text":"The principal parameters for elliptic curve cryptography are the elliptic curve E and a designated point G on E called the base point. The base point has order n, which is a large prime. The number of points on the curve is hn for some integer h (the cofactor), which is not divisible by n.  For efficiency reasons, it is desirable to have the cofactor be as small as possible. All of the curves given below have cofactors 1, 2, or 4. As a result, the private and public keys for a curve are approximately the same length. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":2019,"index":2019,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(197), start: 18, end: 19 })","issues":"[]","pages":[96],"side":0,"text":"D.1.1.2 Choice of Underlying Fields "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":2020,"index":2020,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(197), start: 19, end: 20 })","issues":"[]","pages":[96],"side":0,"text":"For each key length, two kinds of fields are provided. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":2021,"index":2021,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(197), start: 20, end: 21 })","issues":"[]","pages":[96],"side":0,"text":"• A prime field is the field GF(p), which contains a prime number p of elements. The "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":2022,"index":2022,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(197), start: 21, end: 23 })","issues":"[]","pages":[96],"side":0,"text":"elements of this field are the integers modulo p, and the field arithmetic is implemented in terms of the arithmetic of integers modulo p. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":2023,"index":2023,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(197), start: 23, end: 24 })","issues":"[]","pages":[96],"side":0,"text":"• A binary field is the field GF(2m), which contains 2m elements for some m (called the "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":2024,"index":2024,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(197), start: 24, end: 26 })","issues":"[]","pages":[96],"side":0,"text":"degree of the field). The elements of this field are the bit strings of length m, and the field arithmetic is implemented in terms of operations on the bits. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":2025,"index":2025,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(197), start: 26, end: 33 })","issues":"[]","pages":[96],"side":0,"text":"The security strengths for five ranges of the bit length of n is provided in SP 800-57. For the field GF(p), the security strength is dependent on the length of the binary expansion of p. For the field GF(2m), the security strength is dependent on the value of m. Table E-1 provides the bit lengths of the various underlying fields of the curves provided in this appendix. Column 1 lists the ranges for the bit length of n (also see Table 1 in Section 6.1.1). Column 2 identifies the value of p used for the curves over prime fields, where len(p) is the length of the binary expansion of the integer p. Column 3 provides the value of m for the curves over binary fields. "}
