ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":460,"index":460,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(58), start: 0, end: 1 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":1,"text":"FIPS 186-5 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":461,"index":461,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(58), start: 1, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":1,"text":"DIGITAL SIGNATURE STANDARD (DSS) "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":462,"index":462,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(58), start: 2, end: 3 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":1,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":463,"index":463,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(58), start: 3, end: 4 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":1,"text":"4 The Digital Signature Algorithm (DSA) "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":464,"index":464,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(58), start: 4, end: 8 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":1,"text":"Prior versions of this standard specified the DSA. This standard no longer approves the DSA for digital signature generation. However, the DSA may be used to verify signatures generated prior to the implementation date of this standard. See FIPS 186-4 [7] for the specifications for the DSA. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":465,"index":465,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(58), start: 8, end: 9 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":1,"text":"5. The RSA Digital Signature Algorithm "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":466,"index":466,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(58), start: 9, end: 13 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":1,"text":"The use of the RSA algorithm for digital signature generation and verification is specified in IETF RFC 8017, Public Key Cryptography Standard (PKCS) #1: RSA Cryptography Specifications Version 2.2 [2]. This standard imposes additional restrictions, which are enumerated below (see Section 5.4). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":467,"index":467,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(58), start: 13, end: 14 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":1,"text":"5.1 RSA Key Pair Generation "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":468,"index":468,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(58), start: 14, end: 42 })","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":1,"text":"An RSA digital signature key pair consists of an RSA private key, which is used to compute a digital signature, and an RSA public key, which is used to verify a digital signature. An RSA digital signature key pair shall not be used for other purposes (e.g., key establishment). An RSA public key consists of a modulus n, which is the product of two positive prime integers p and q (i.e., n = pq) and a public key exponent e. Thus, the RSA public key is the pair of values (n, e) and is used to verify digital signatures. The size of an RSA key pair is commonly considered to be the length of the modulus n in bits (nlen). The corresponding RSA private key consists of the same modulus n and a private key exponent d that depends on n and the public key exponent e. Thus, the RSA private key is the pair of values (n, d) and is used to generate digital signatures. Note that an alternative method for representing (n, d) using the Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT) is allowed (see Sections 6.2 and 6.3 of SP 800-56B [14]). In order to provide security for the digital signature process, the two integers p and q and the private key exponent d shall be kept secret. Guidance on the protection of these values is provided in SP 800-57, Part 1. The modulus n and the public key exponent e may be made known to anyone. This standard specifies the use of a modulus whose bit length is an even integer and greater than or equal to 2048 bits. Furthermore, this standard specifies that p and q be of the same bit length – namely, half the bit length of n. The maximum security strength of RSA schemes associated with the bit length of the modulus is specified in NIST SP 800-57, Part 1 [12]. Approved hash functions shall be used during the generation of key pairs and digital signatures. When used during the generation of an RSA key pair (as specified in this standard), the length in bits of the hash function output block shall meet or exceed the security strength associated with the bit length of the modulus n (see SP 800-57, Part 1). The security strength associated with the RSA digital signature process is no greater than the minimum of the security strength associated with the bit length of the modulus and the security strength of the hash function that is employed (see Table 3 in SP 800-57, Part 1). The (maximum) security strengths associated with certain RSA modulus lengths and approved hash "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":469,"index":469,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[24],"side":1,"text":"16 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":470,"index":470,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(59), start: 0, end: 1 })","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":1,"text":"FIPS 186-5 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":471,"index":471,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(59), start: 1, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":1,"text":"DIGITAL SIGNATURE STANDARD (DSS) "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":472,"index":472,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(59), start: 2, end: 24 })","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":1,"text":" functions used during the digital signature process are provided in SP 800-57, Part 1. Both the security strength of the hash function used for the digital signature and the security strength associated with the bit length of the modulus n shall meet or exceed the security strength required for the digital signature process. The security strength of the hash function used should be greater than or equal to the security strength of the modulus since, otherwise, the security strength of the digital signature process is reduced to a level no greater than that provided by the hash function. A CA should use a modulus whose length nlen is equal to or greater than the bit-length of every modulus used by its subscribers. For example, if the subscribers are using nlen = 2048, then the CA should use nlen ≥ 2048. SP 800-57, Parts 1 and 3 [12, 15], provide further information about comparable security strength guidance. Criteria for the generation of RSA key pairs are provided in Appendix A.1.1. When RSA parameters are randomly generated (i.e., the primes p and q and, optionally, the public key exponent e), they shall be generated using an approved random bit generator. The (pseudo) random bits produced by the random bit generator shall be used as seeds for generating RSA parameters. Prime number generation seeds shall be kept secret or destroyed when the modulus n is computed. If any prime number generation seed is retained (e.g., to regenerate the RSA modulus n or as evidence that the generated prime factors p and q were generated in compliance with this standard), then the seed shall be kept secret and shall be protected. The strength of this protection shall be (at least) equivalent to the protection required for the associated private key. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":473,"index":473,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(59), start: 24, end: 25 })","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":1,"text":"5.2 RSA Key Pair Management "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":474,"index":474,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(59), start: 25, end: 27 })","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":1,"text":"Guidance on the protection of key pairs is provided in SP 800-57, Part 1. The secure use of digital signatures depends on the management of an entity’s digital signature key pair as follows: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":475,"index":475,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(59), start: 27, end: 28 })","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":1,"text":"1. The private key shall be used only for signature generation, as specified in this standard, "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":476,"index":476,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(59), start: 28, end: 30 })","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":1,"text":"and shall be kept secret. The public key shall be used only for signature verification, as specified in this standard, and may be made public. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":477,"index":477,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(59), start: 30, end: 31 })","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":1,"text":"2. An intended signatory shall have assurance of possession of the private key prior to or "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":478,"index":478,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(59), start: 31, end: 32 })","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":1,"text":"concurrently with using it to generate a digital signature (see Section 3.1). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":479,"index":479,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(59), start: 32, end: 34 })","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":1,"text":"3. A private key shall be protected from unauthorized access, disclosure, and modification. 4. A public key shall be protected from unauthorized modification (including substitution). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":480,"index":480,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(59), start: 34, end: 35 })","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":1,"text":"For example, public key certificates that are signed by a CA may provide such protection. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":481,"index":481,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(59), start: 35, end: 37 })","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":1,"text":"5. A verifier shall be assured of a binding between the public key and the key pair owner (see Section 3). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":482,"index":482,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(59), start: 37, end: 38 })","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":1,"text":"6. A verifier shall obtain public keys in a trusted manner (e.g., from a certificate signed by a "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":483,"index":483,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(59), start: 38, end: 41 })","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":1,"text":"CA that the entity trusts or directly from the intended or claimed signatory, provided that the entity is trusted by the verifier and can be authenticated as the source of the signed information that is to be verified). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":484,"index":484,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(59), start: 41, end: 42 })","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":1,"text":"7. Verifiers shall be assured that the claimed signatory is the key pair owner and that the "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":485,"index":485,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[25],"side":1,"text":"17 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":486,"index":486,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(60), start: 0, end: 1 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":1,"text":"FIPS 186-5 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":487,"index":487,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(60), start: 1, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":1,"text":"DIGITAL SIGNATURE STANDARD (DSS) "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":488,"index":488,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(60), start: 2, end: 3 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":1,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":489,"index":489,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(60), start: 3, end: 6 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":1,"text":"owner possessed the correct private key at the time the signature was generated (i.e., the private key that is associated with the public key used to verify the digital signature) (see Section 3.3). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":490,"index":490,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(60), start: 6, end: 8 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":1,"text":"8. A signatory and a verifier shall have assurance of the validity of the public key (see Sections 3.1 and 3.3). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":491,"index":491,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(60), start: 8, end: 9 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":1,"text":"5.3 Assurances "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":492,"index":492,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(60), start: 9, end: 11 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":1,"text":"The intended signatory shall have assurances as specified in Section 3.1. Prior to accepting a digital signature as valid, the verifier shall have assurances as specified in Section 3.3. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":493,"index":493,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(60), start: 11, end: 12 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":1,"text":"5.4 PKCS #1 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":494,"index":494,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(60), start: 12, end: 13 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":1,"text":"IETF RFC 8017, PKCS #1: RSA Cryptography Specifications Version 2.2 [1], defines "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":495,"index":495,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(60), start: 13, end: 17 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":1,"text":"mechanisms for encrypting and signing data using the RSA algorithm. In particular, it specifies two digital signature processes and corresponding formats: RSASSA-PKCS1-v1.5 and RSASSA-PSS. Both of these signature schemes are approved for use, but additional constraints are imposed in addition to those specified in the IETF RFC. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":496,"index":496,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(60), start: 17, end: 19 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":1,"text":"(a) Implementations that generate RSA key pairs shall use the criteria and methods in Appendix B.3 to generate those key pairs. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":497,"index":497,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(60), start: 19, end: 20 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":1,"text":"(b) For RSASSA-PSS, either an approved hash function or XOF (extendable-output "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":498,"index":498,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(60), start: 20, end: 24 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":1,"text":"function) shall be used as the function “Hash” in Sections 9.1.1 and 9.1.2 of RFC 8017. Approved XOFs are SHAKE128 and SHAKE256, which are specified in FIPS 202. When SHAKE128 or SHAKE256 is used as the function “Hash,” the output length shall be 256 or 512 bits, respectively. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":499,"index":499,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(60), start: 24, end: 25 })","issues":"[]","pages":[26],"side":1,"text":"(c) For RSASSA-PKCS-v1.5, only approved hash functions shall be used. "}
