ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":671,"index":671,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(83), start: 5, end: 6 })","issues":"[]","pages":[35],"side":1,"text":"7.4 EdDSA Key Pair Generation "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":672,"index":672,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(83), start: 6, end: 8 })","issues":"[]","pages":[35],"side":1,"text":"EdDSA public keys have exactly b bits, and EdDSA signatures have exactly 2b bits. The value b is a multiple of 8, therefore, public key and signature lengths are an integral number of octets. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":673,"index":673,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[35],"side":1,"text":"27 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":674,"index":674,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 0, end: 1 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"FIPS 186-5 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":675,"index":675,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 1, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"DIGITAL SIGNATURE STANDARD (DSS) "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":676,"index":676,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 2, end: 6 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":" For Ed25519, b is 256, so the private key is 32 octets. For Ed448, b is 456, and the private key is 57 octets. See IETF RFC 8032. The method to generate the public-private key (d, Q) pair is provided in Appendix A.2.3. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":677,"index":677,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 6, end: 7 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"7.5 Key Pair Management "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":678,"index":678,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 7, end: 10 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"The secure use of digital signatures depends on the management of an entity’s digital signature key pair. Key pair management requirements for EdDSA are the same as for ECDSA, which are provided in Section 6.2.2. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":679,"index":679,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 10, end: 11 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"7.6 EdDSA Signature Generation "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":680,"index":680,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 11, end: 14 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"EdDSA signatures are deterministic. The signature is generated using the hash of the private key and the message using the procedure below or an equivalent process.  "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":681,"index":681,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 14, end: 16 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"Inputs:  "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":682,"index":682,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 16, end: 20 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"1. Bit string M to be signed 2. Valid public-private key pair (d, Q) for domain parameters D 3. H: SHA-512 for Ed25519 or SHAKE256 for Ed448 4. For Ed448, a string context set by the signer and verifier with a maximum length of 255 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":683,"index":683,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 20, end: 21 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"octets; by default, context is the empty string "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":684,"index":684,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 21, end: 23 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":" Output: The signature R || S, where R is an encoding of a point and S is an octet string of a given "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":685,"index":685,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 23, end: 24 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"length of a little-endian encoded value. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":686,"index":686,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 24, end: 25 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":687,"index":687,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 25, end: 26 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"Process: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":688,"index":688,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 26, end: 29 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":" As specified in IETF RFC 8032, the EdDSA signature of a message M under a private key d is defined as the 2b-bit string R || S. The octet strings R and S are derived as follows: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":689,"index":689,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 29, end: 30 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"1. Compute the hash of the private key d, H(d) = (h0, h1, ..., h2b-1) using SHA-512 for "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":690,"index":690,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 30, end: 32 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"Ed25519 and SHAKE256 for Ed448 (H(d)= SHAKE256(d, 912)). H(d) may be precomputed. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":691,"index":691,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 32, end: 33 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"2. Using the second half of the digest hdigest2 = hb || ... || h2b-1, define: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":692,"index":692,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 33, end: 34 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"2.1 For Ed25519, r = SHA-512(hdigest2 || M); Interpret r as a 64-octet little-endian "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":693,"index":693,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 34, end: 35 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"integer. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":694,"index":694,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 35, end: 36 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"2.2 For Ed448, r = SHAKE256(dom4(0, context) || hdigest2 || M, 912). In IETF RFC "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":695,"index":695,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 36, end: 40 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"8032, dom4(f, c) is defined to be (“SigEd448” || octet(f) || octet(octetlength(c)) || c). The string “SigEd448” is in ASCII (8 octets). The value octet(f) is the octet with value f, and octetlength(c) is the number of octets in string c. Interpret r as a 114-octet little-endian integer. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":696,"index":696,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(84), start: 40, end: 43 })","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"3. Compute the point [r]G. The octet string R is the encoding of the point [r]G. 4. Derive s from H(d) as in the key pair generation algorithm. Use octet strings R, Q, and M to define: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":697,"index":697,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[36],"side":1,"text":"28 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":698,"index":698,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 0, end: 1 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":"FIPS 186-5 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":699,"index":699,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 1, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":"DIGITAL SIGNATURE STANDARD (DSS) "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":700,"index":700,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 2, end: 3 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":701,"index":701,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 3, end: 6 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":"4.1 For Ed25519, digest = SHA-512(R || Q || M). 4.2 For Ed448, digest = SHAKE256(dom4(0, context) || R || Q || M, 912). Interpret digest as a little-endian integer. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":702,"index":702,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 6, end: 8 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":"5. Compute S = (r + digest × s) mod n. The octet string S is the encoding of the resultant integer. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":703,"index":703,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 8, end: 9 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":"6. Form the signature as the concatenation of the octet strings R and S. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":704,"index":704,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 9, end: 10 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":"7.7 EdDSA Signature Verification "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":705,"index":705,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 10, end: 12 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":"Inputs:  "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":706,"index":706,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 12, end: 17 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":"1. Message M 2. Signature R || S where R and S are octet strings 3. Purported signature verification key Q that is valid for domain parameters D 4. For Ed448, a string context set by the signer and verifier with a maximum length of 255 octets; by default, context is the empty string "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":707,"index":707,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 17, end: 19 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":" Output: Accept or reject the signature over M as originating from the owner of public key Q. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":708,"index":708,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 19, end: 20 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":"Process: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":709,"index":709,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 20, end: 22 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":" 1. Decode the first half of the signature as a point R and the second half of the signature as an "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":710,"index":710,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 22, end: 24 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":"integer t. Verify that the integer t is in the range of 0 ≤ t < n. Decode the public key Q into a point Q’. If any of the decodings fail, output “reject”. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":711,"index":711,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 24, end: 25 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":"2. Using the established hash function or XOF, "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":712,"index":712,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 25, end: 28 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":"2.1 For Ed25519, compute digest = SHA-512(R || Q || M). 2.2 For Ed448, compute digest = SHAKE256(dom4(0, context) || R || Q || M, 912) Interpret digest as a little-endian integer u. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":713,"index":713,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 28, end: 29 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":"3. Check that the verification equation [2ct]G = [2c]R + [2cu]Q’ holds. It’s sufficient, but not "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":714,"index":714,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 29, end: 31 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":"required, to instead check [t]G = R + [u]Q’. Output “reject” if verification fails; output “accept” otherwise. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":715,"index":715,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 31, end: 32 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":716,"index":716,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 32, end: 33 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":"7.8 The Prehash Edwards-Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (HashEdDSA) "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":717,"index":717,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(85), start: 33, end: 40 })","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":"The Prehash Edwards-Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (HashEdDSA) is a version of the EdDSA digital signature scheme. The main difference is that HashEdDSA generates a signature on the hash of the message M, unlike EdDSA which signs the message M directly. The domain parameters and key generation for HashEdDSA are exactly the same as for EdDSA with the two options denoted Ed25519ph and Ed448ph. Below are the signature generation and verification processes for Ed25519ph and Ed448ph. Section 7.8.3 explains potential reasons for choosing between HashEdDSA and EdDSA. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":718,"index":718,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[37],"side":1,"text":"29 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":719,"index":719,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 0, end: 1 })","issues":"[]","pages":[38],"side":1,"text":"FIPS 186-5 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":720,"index":720,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 1, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[38],"side":1,"text":"DIGITAL SIGNATURE STANDARD (DSS) "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":721,"index":721,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 2, end: 4 })","issues":"[]","pages":[38],"side":1,"text":"  "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":722,"index":722,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 4, end: 5 })","issues":"[]","pages":[38],"side":1,"text":"7.8.1 HashEdDSA Signature Generation "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":723,"index":723,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 5, end: 7 })","issues":"[]","pages":[38],"side":1,"text":"Inputs:  "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":724,"index":724,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 7, end: 12 })","issues":"[]","pages":[38],"side":1,"text":"1. Bit string M to be signed 2. Valid public-private key pair (d, Q) for domain parameters D 3. H: SHA-512 for Ed25519ph or SHAKE256 for Ed448ph 4. A string context set by the signer and verifier with a maximum length of 255 octets; by default, context is the empty string "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":725,"index":725,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 12, end: 14 })","issues":"[]","pages":[38],"side":1,"text":" Output: The signature R || S, where R is an encoding of a point, and S is a little-endian encoded "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":726,"index":726,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 14, end: 15 })","issues":"[]","pages":[38],"side":1,"text":"value. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":727,"index":727,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 15, end: 16 })","issues":"[]","pages":[38],"side":1,"text":"Process: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":728,"index":728,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 16, end: 25 })","issues":"[]","pages":[38],"side":1,"text":" As specified in IETF RFC 8032, the HashEdDSA signature of a message M under a private key d is defined as the 2b-bit string R || S. The octet strings R and S are derived as follows:  1. Compute h(M) = SHA-512(M) for Ed25519ph or h(M) = SHAKE256(M, 512) for Ed448ph. 2. Compute the hash of the private key d, H(d) = (h0, h1, ..., h2b-1) using SHA-512 for Ed25519ph and SHAKE256 for Ed448ph (H(d) = SHAKE256(d, 912) ). H(d) may be precomputed. 3. Using the second half of the digest hdigest2 = hb || ... || h2b-1, define: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":729,"index":729,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 25, end: 26 })","issues":"[]","pages":[38],"side":1,"text":"3.1 For Ed25519ph, r = SHA-512(dom2(1, context ) || hdigest2 || h(M)); r will be 64 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":730,"index":730,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 26, end: 32 })","issues":"[]","pages":[38],"side":1,"text":"octets. In IETF RFC 8032, dom2(f, c) is defined to be the octet string (“SigEd25519 no Ed25519 collisions” || octet(f) || octet(octetlength(c)) || c). The string “SigEd25519 no Ed25519 collisions” is in ASCII (32 octets). The value octet(f) is the octet with value f, and octetlength(c) is the number of octets in string c. The string context is set by the signer and verifier (maximum is 255 octets) with the empty string as default. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":731,"index":731,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 32, end: 33 })","issues":"[]","pages":[38],"side":1,"text":"3.2 For Ed448ph, r = SHAKE256(dom4(1, context) || hdigest2 || h(M), 912), where "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":732,"index":732,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 33, end: 35 })","issues":"[]","pages":[38],"side":1,"text":"context is set by the signer and verifier (maximum is 255 octets) with the empty string as default. Recall dom4(f,c) is defined in Section 7.6. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":733,"index":733,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 35, end: 36 })","issues":"[]","pages":[38],"side":1,"text":"In either instance, interpret r as a little-endian integer. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":734,"index":734,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 36, end: 38 })","issues":"[]","pages":[38],"side":1,"text":"4. Compute the point [r]G. The octet string R is the encoding of the point [r]G. 5. Use octet strings R, Q, and h(M) to define: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":735,"index":735,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(86), start: 38, end: 41 })","issues":"[]","pages":[38],"side":1,"text":"5.1 For Ed25519ph, digest = SHA-512(dom2(1, context ) || R || Q || h(M)). 5.2 For Ed448ph, digest = SHAKE256(dom4(1, context ) || R || Q || h(M), 912). Interpret digest as a little-endian integer. "}
