ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":743,"index":743,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 7, end: 8 })","issues":"[]","pages":[39],"side":1,"text":"7.8.2 HashEdDSA Signature Verification "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":744,"index":744,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 8, end: 10 })","issues":"[]","pages":[39],"side":1,"text":"Inputs:  "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":745,"index":745,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 10, end: 15 })","issues":"[]","pages":[39],"side":1,"text":"1. Message M 2. Signature R || S where R and S are octet strings 3. Purported signature verification key Q that is valid for domain parameters D 4. A string context set by the signer and verifier with a maximum length of 255 octets; by default, context is the empty string "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":746,"index":746,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 15, end: 17 })","issues":"[]","pages":[39],"side":1,"text":" Output: Accept or reject the signature over M as originating from the owner of public key Q. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":747,"index":747,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 17, end: 18 })","issues":"[]","pages":[39],"side":1,"text":"Process: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":748,"index":748,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 18, end: 20 })","issues":"[]","pages":[39],"side":1,"text":" 1. Decode the first half of the signature as a point R and the second half of the signature as an "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":749,"index":749,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 20, end: 22 })","issues":"[]","pages":[39],"side":1,"text":"integer s. Verify that the integer s is in the range of 0 ≤ s < n. Decode the public key Q into a point Q’. If any of the decodings fail, output “reject”. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":750,"index":750,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 22, end: 23 })","issues":"[]","pages":[39],"side":1,"text":"2. Form the bit string HashData as the concatenation of the octet strings R, Q, and h(M) (i.e., "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":751,"index":751,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 23, end: 25 })","issues":"[]","pages":[39],"side":1,"text":"HashData = R || Q || h(M)) with h(M) = SHA-512(M) for Ed25519ph or h(M) = SHAKE256(M, 512) for Ed448ph. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":752,"index":752,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 25, end: 26 })","issues":"[]","pages":[39],"side":1,"text":"3. Using SHA-512 or SHAKE256, "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":753,"index":753,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 26, end: 29 })","issues":"[]","pages":[39],"side":1,"text":"3.1 For Ed25519ph, compute digest = SHA-512(dom2(1, context ) || HashData). 3.2 For Ed448ph, compute digest = SHAKE256(dom4(1, context ) || HashData, 912). Interpret digest as a little-endian integer t. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":754,"index":754,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 29, end: 30 })","issues":"[]","pages":[39],"side":1,"text":"4. Check that the verification equation [2c s]G = [2c]R + [2ct]Q’ holds. It is sufficient, but not "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":755,"index":755,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 30, end: 32 })","issues":"[]","pages":[39],"side":1,"text":"required, to instead check [s]G = R + [t]Q’. Output “reject” if verification fails; output “accept” otherwise. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":756,"index":756,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 32, end: 33 })","issues":"[]","pages":[39],"side":1,"text":"7.8.3 Differences between EdDSA and HashEdDSA "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":757,"index":757,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(87), start: 33, end: 39 })","issues":"[]","pages":[39],"side":1,"text":"A difference between EdDSA and its prehash version is that HashEdDSA generates a signature on the hash of the message M, unlike EdDSA which signs the message M directly. As a result, EdDSA must hash the message twice while HashEdDSA only needs to hash once. Another difference is that with EdDSA the whole message M must be either buffered or read from its storage twice. Thus, for long messages, it is expected that HashEdDSA will have better performance. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":758,"index":758,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[39],"side":1,"text":"31 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":759,"index":759,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(88), start: 0, end: 1 })","issues":"[]","pages":[40],"side":1,"text":"FIPS 186-5 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":760,"index":760,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(88), start: 1, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[40],"side":1,"text":"DIGITAL SIGNATURE STANDARD (DSS) "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":761,"index":761,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(88), start: 2, end: 6 })","issues":"[]","pages":[40],"side":1,"text":" It should be noted that even if it is feasible to compute collisions on the hash function (or XOF) used, there is believed to be no adverse effect on the security of EdDSA. This property is not true for HashEdDSA since collisions can result in forged messages. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":762,"index":762,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(88), start: 6, end: 7 })","issues":"[]","pages":[40],"side":1,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":763,"index":763,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(88), start: 7, end: 8 })","issues":"[]","pages":[40],"side":1,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":764,"index":764,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[40],"side":1,"text":"32 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":765,"index":765,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 0, end: 1 })","issues":"[]","pages":[41],"side":1,"text":"FIPS 186-5 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":766,"index":766,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 1, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[41],"side":1,"text":"DIGITAL SIGNATURE STANDARD (DSS) "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":767,"index":767,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 2, end: 3 })","issues":"[]","pages":[41],"side":1,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":768,"index":768,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 3, end: 4 })","issues":"[]","pages":[41],"side":1,"text":"APPENDIX A: Key Pair Generation "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":769,"index":769,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 4, end: 18 })","issues":"[]","pages":[41],"side":1,"text":"Discrete logarithm cryptography (DLC) is divided into finite field cryptography (FFC) and elliptic curve cryptography (ECC). The difference between the two is the type of mathematics that is used. DSA is an example of FFC, and ECDSA is an example of ECC. Other examples of DLC are the Diffie-Hellman and MQV key agreement algorithms, which have both FFC and ECC forms. The most common example of integer factorization cryptography (IFC) is RSA. This appendix specifies methods for the generation of ECC key pairs, secret numbers, and IFC key pairs. All generation methods require the use of an approved, properly instantiated deterministic random bit generator (DRBG). The DRBG shall have a security strength equal to or greater than the security strength associated with the key pairs and secret numbers to be generated. See SP 800-57, Part 1, for guidance on security strengths and key sizes. This appendix does not indicate the required conversions between bit strings and integers. When required by a process in this appendix, the conversion shall be accomplished as specified in Appendix B.2. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":770,"index":770,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 18, end: 20 })","issues":"[]","pages":[41],"side":1,"text":"A.1 IFC Key Pair Generation A.1.1 Criteria for IFC Key Pairs "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":771,"index":771,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 20, end: 24 })","issues":"[]","pages":[41],"side":1,"text":"Key pairs for IFC consist of a public key (n, e) and a private key (n, d) where n is the modulus and product of two prime numbers, p and q. The security of IFC depends on the quality and secrecy of these primes and the private exponent d. The primes p and q shall be generated using one of the following methods: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":772,"index":772,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 24, end: 26 })","issues":"[]","pages":[41],"side":1,"text":"A. Both p and q are randomly generated prime numbers (random primes), where p and q shall both be either: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":773,"index":773,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 26, end: 28 })","issues":"[]","pages":[41],"side":1,"text":"1. Provable primes (see Appendix A.1.2) or 2. Probable primes (see Appendix A.1.3). "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":774,"index":774,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 28, end: 30 })","issues":"[]","pages":[41],"side":1,"text":"Using methods 1 and 2, p and q with lengths equal to one half the length of the modulus are generated. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":775,"index":775,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 30, end: 32 })","issues":"[]","pages":[41],"side":1,"text":"B. Both p and q are randomly generated prime numbers that satisfy the following additional conditions (Primes with Conditions): "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":776,"index":776,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 32, end: 36 })","issues":"[]","pages":[41],"side":1,"text":"• (p–1) has a prime factor p1 • (p+1) has a prime factor p2 • (q–1) has a prime factor q1 • (q+1) has a prime factor q2 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":777,"index":777,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(89), start: 36, end: 38 })","issues":"[]","pages":[41],"side":1,"text":"where p1, p2, q1 and q2 are called auxiliary primes of p and q. Using this method, one of the following cases shall apply: "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":778,"index":778,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[41],"side":1,"text":"33 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":779,"index":779,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(90), start: 0, end: 1 })","issues":"[]","pages":[42],"side":1,"text":"FIPS 186-5 "}
