ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1562,"index":1562,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(144), start: 7, end: 8 })","issues":"[]","pages":[73],"side":1,"text":"B.8 Sieve Procedure "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1563,"index":1563,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(144), start: 8, end: 16 })","issues":"[]","pages":[73],"side":1,"text":"A sieve procedure is described as follows: given a sequence of integers Y0, Y0 + 1, … , Y0 + J, a sieve will identify the integers in the sequence that are divisible by primes up to some selected limit. Note that the definitions of the mathematical symbols in this process (e.g., h, L, M, p) are internal to this process only and should not be confused with their use elsewhere in this standard. Start by selecting a factor base of all the primes pj from 2 up to some selected limit L. The value of L is arbitrary and may be determined by computer limitations. A good, typical value of L would be anywhere from 103 to 105. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1564,"index":1564,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[73],"side":1,"text":"65 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1565,"index":1565,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(145), start: 0, end: 1 })","issues":"[]","pages":[74],"side":1,"text":"FIPS 186-5 "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1566,"index":1566,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(145), start: 1, end: 2 })","issues":"[]","pages":[74],"side":1,"text":"DIGITAL SIGNATURE STANDARD (DSS) "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1567,"index":1567,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(145), start: 2, end: 3 })","issues":"[]","pages":[74],"side":1,"text":" "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1568,"index":1568,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(145), start: 3, end: 6 })","issues":"[]","pages":[74],"side":1,"text":"1. Compute Sj =Y0 mod pj for all pj in the factor base. 2. Initialize an array of length J + 1 to zero. 3. Starting at Y0 – Sj + pj , let every pjth element of the array be set to 1 (including the first "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1569,"index":1569,"interval":"None","issues":"[]","pages":[74],"side":1,"text":"The 4a. rrasWym hcaelalnn pfbriien imesieht ehaden,rd i afi stb htithe eairrnerdfaoeyr xfe i o cro ofc mothpmeo psaairtcreta.n y e isss sweth teon 1m, ethmeonr y𝑌𝑌 0is+ s𝑝𝑝ma−ll1 oirs ad ibvyitsei balrer abyy fsoor me "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1570,"index":1570,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(146), start: 0, end: 1 })","issues":"[]","pages":[74],"side":1,"text":"element of the array if Sj = 0). Do this for the entire length of the array and for every j. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1571,"index":1571,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(146), start: 1, end: 10 })","issues":"[]","pages":[74],"side":1,"text":"speed when memory is readily available. There is no need to sieve the entire sieve interval at once. The array can be partitioned into suitably small pieces, sieving each piece before going on to the next piece. When finished, every location with the value 0 is a candidate for prime testing. The amount of work for this procedure is approximately M log log L, where M is the length of the sieve interval. This is a very efficient procedure for removing composite candidates for primality testing. If L = 105, the sieve will remove about 96 % of all composites. In some cases, rather than having a set of consecutive integers to sieve, the set of integers to be tested consists of integers lying in an arithmetic progression Y0, Y0 + h, Y0 + 2h, …, Y0 + Jh, where h is large and not divisible by any primes in the factor base. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1572,"index":1572,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(146), start: 10, end: 12 })","issues":"[]","pages":[74],"side":1,"text":"1. Select a factor base and initialize an array of length J + 1 to 0. 2. Compute Sj =Y0 mod pj for all pj in the factor base. "}
ISSUE12_BLOCK {"id":1573,"index":1573,"interval":"Some(TrustedRunInterval { run_id: TrustedRunId(146), start: 12, end: 13 })","issues":"[]","pages":[74],"side":1,"text":"3. Compute Tj = h mod pj and r = – Sj Tj – 1 mod pj. "}
