ISSUE12_VIEW {"blocks":[768],"index":573,"kind":"Untrusted(768)","side":1,"tokens":"APPENDIX A: Key Pair Generation "}
ISSUE12_VIEW {"blocks":[769],"index":574,"kind":"Untrusted(769)","side":1,"tokens":"Discrete logarithm cryptography (DLC) is divided into finite field cryptography (FFC) and elliptic curve cryptography (ECC). The difference between the two is the type of mathematics that is used. DSA is an example of FFC, and ECDSA is an example of ECC. Other examples of DLC are the Diffie-Hellman and MQV key agreement algorithms, which have both FFC and ECC forms. The most common example of integer factorization cryptography (IFC) is RSA. This appendix specifies methods for the generation of ECC key pairs, secret numbers, and IFC key pairs. All generation methods require the use of an approved, properly instantiated deterministic random bit generator (DRBG). The DRBG shall have a security strength equal to or greater than the security strength associated with the key pairs and secret numbers to be generated. See SP 800-57, Part 1, for guidance on security strengths and key sizes. This appendix does not indicate the required conversions between bit strings and integers. When required by a process in this appendix, the conversion shall be accomplished as specified in Appendix B.2. "}
ISSUE12_VIEW {"blocks":[770],"index":575,"kind":"Untrusted(770)","side":1,"tokens":"A.1 IFC Key Pair Generation A.1.1 Criteria for IFC Key Pairs "}
ISSUE12_VIEW {"blocks":[771],"index":576,"kind":"Untrusted(771)","side":1,"tokens":"Key pairs for IFC consist of a public key (n, e) and a private key (n, d) where n is the modulus and product of two prime numbers, p and q. The security of IFC depends on the quality and secrecy of these primes and the private exponent d. The primes p and q shall be generated using one of the following methods: "}
ISSUE12_VIEW {"blocks":[772],"index":577,"kind":"Untrusted(772)","side":1,"tokens":"A. Both p and q are randomly generated prime numbers (random primes), where p and q shall both be either: "}
ISSUE12_VIEW {"blocks":[773],"index":578,"kind":"Untrusted(773)","side":1,"tokens":"1. Provable primes (see Appendix A.1.2) or 2. Probable primes (see Appendix A.1.3). "}
ISSUE12_VIEW {"blocks":[774],"index":579,"kind":"Untrusted(774)","side":1,"tokens":"Using methods 1 and 2, p and q with lengths equal to one half the length of the modulus are generated. "}
ISSUE12_VIEW {"blocks":[775],"index":580,"kind":"Untrusted(775)","side":1,"tokens":"B. Both p and q are randomly generated prime numbers that satisfy the following additional conditions (Primes with Conditions): "}
ISSUE12_VIEW {"blocks":[776],"index":581,"kind":"Untrusted(776)","side":1,"tokens":"• (p–1) has a prime factor p1 • (p+1) has a prime factor p2 • (q–1) has a prime factor q1 • (q+1) has a prime factor q2 "}
ISSUE12_VIEW {"blocks":[777],"index":582,"kind":"Untrusted(777)","side":1,"tokens":"where p1, p2, q1 and q2 are called auxiliary primes of p and q. Using this method, one of the following cases shall apply: "}
ISSUE12_VIEW {"blocks":[778],"index":583,"kind":"Untrusted(778)","side":1,"tokens":"33 "}
